<!--
    IconGeometries.xaml - Kaloyan's own SVG icon set, ported to WPF Geometry resources.

    Source SVGs live alongside this file (add/mic/send/stop/stop_mic/tools, each _light/_dark).
    Each Geometry below is that SVG's own <path> "d" data, copied verbatim except for two
    mechanical fixups needed because WPF's Path mini-language is close to but not identical to
    SVG's:

      1. A leading "F1 " fill-rule directive on every one, since SVG defaults to a nonzero fill
         rule but WPF's Geometry.Parse defaults to EvenOdd - several of these icons (e.g. the
         plus-in-square "add" glyph) rely on nonzero to render their cutouts as holes rather than
         inverting.
      2. A space inserted wherever an SVG optimizer glued two numbers together across a second
         decimal point ("3.965.282", the second ".282" meaning "0.282") - WPF's number tokenizer
         does not support that specific SVG compression trick and reads it as one malformed token
         without the extra separator.

    A multi-<path> SVG (the two mic icons) has both "d" strings concatenated into one Geometry,
    which is exactly how multiple subpaths already work in this format ("M...Z M...Z").

    Naming: "_Light"/"_Dark" here means the SAME thing the source SVG filenames mean - the icon's
    OWN color scheme, not which VS theme it belongs on. Kaloyan's own note (2026-09-05): the
    "_light" variant is a light-colored glyph, meant to sit on a DARK Visual Studio theme; the
    "_dark" variant is a dark-colored glyph, meant to sit on a LIGHT Visual Studio theme - backwards
    from what the suffix alone would suggest. See Controls/ThemeService.cs for where that mapping
    is actually applied.
-->
